<title>JAPAN: EU urges Japan to hold talk on pork tariffs.</title>
<headline>EU urges Japan to hold talk on pork tariffs.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1997-01-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Union (EU) has asked the Japanese government to hold negotiations to review Japan's pork import tariff scheme, Agricultural Ministry officials said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The EU sent a letter last week to Japan's envoy in Geneva, Nobutoshi Akao, warning that the scheme was damaging European meat exports, the officials said.</p>
<p>&quot;No decision on a place or a schedule for the talks has been made yet,&quot; one of the officials said.</p>
<p>The Japanese government raised its emergency tariff on imported pork for a three-month period from January 1 to protect local producers from an influx of imports.</p>
<p>Japan first imposed the emergency tariff last July 1 due to rising imports.</p>
<p>Under the new scheme, the duty on pork carcasses was raised to 6.4 percent from 4.8 percent for imports with a value greater than 565.70 yen per kg. It also raised the threshold for assessing the duty from the prior 557.19 yen per kg.</p>
<p>-- Tokyo Commodity Desk +81-3-3432-7391</p>
